WITH ins AS (INSERT INTO sais_resource_deadlines (resource_id, appeal_deadline, continued_benefits_deadline, hearing_request_deadline, reconsideration_deadline, judicial_review_deadline, source_file, source_hash8) VALUES 
('SAIS-EC-006-01', 'Not an appeal posture. This step gates route selection -- reports filed with the wrong body are commonly closed rather than forwarded.', 'If the older adult is in immediate danger -- unattended, without food, water, heat, or needed medication -- call 911 first and APS second.', 'Call Adult Protective Services in every setting. Any person may report in every state, anonymously, with statutory good faith immunity.', 'Where a paid caregiver or agency is involved, request the service plan and the visit or task records. These define what was contracted and are the measuring stick for the neglect allegation.', 'Not applicable at this stage. Setting determines which downstream deadlines apply -- discharge hearings in facilities, agency licensing appeals, or civil limitations periods.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-006-02', 'APS findings are generally not appealable by a reporter. A person named in a substantiated finding commonly has an administrative appeal right -- state-specific, commonly 30 days from notice.', 'APS involvement does not remove the older adult''s right to refuse services if they have capacity. A competent adult may decline intervention, and APS will generally document and close. This is autonomy, not failure -- see the Elder Self-Neglect pipeline.', 'Report by phone for speed, follow in writing, and ask for the intake or report number. Emergency reports commonly draw a response within 24 hours.', 'If the report is screened out, ask in writing what additional information would change the screening decision, and re-report when conditions change. Repeated reports establish a pattern APS tracks.', 'A civil claim against a paid caregiver or agency runs on the state personal injury limitations period, commonly 1-3 years. Criminal neglect statutes vary and are frequently longer.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-006-03', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Where records access is refused or delayed, that is separately enforceable through HHS Office for Civil Rights at no cost.', 'Preserve photographs with dates, keep any discarded medication packaging, and keep a written log. Physical conditions improve or worsen and the contemporaneous record is what remains.', 'HIPAA records response: generally 30 days, extendable once by 30 days with written notice. Request prior-year records in the same request so the trajectory is captured.', 'If records are produced incomplete, request an index of what was withheld and the basis for each withholding. Ask specifically for nursing notes and weight records, which are commonly omitted.', 'Civil limitations periods run from injury or discovery, commonly 1-3 years. Where an older adult lacks capacity, tolling rules may apply -- verify rather than assuming the ordinary deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-006-04', 'Agency complaint closure appeal: state-specific, commonly 15-30 days. Request supervisory review in writing where a complaint is closed without an on-site investigation.', 'Notify the agency in writing that services are inadequate and request an immediate reassessment and revised plan of care. Do not terminate services before an alternative is arranged unless the client is unsafe.', 'Complaint to the state licensing office or state survey agency: file at any time. Where the client is at immediate risk, say so explicitly to shorten the response timeline.', 'Request the electronic visit verification records specifically and by name. Most states now require EVV for Medicaid-funded personal care, and the data objectively shows visit start and end times.', 'Where Medicaid or Medicare funds were billed for care not delivered, refer to the state Medicaid Fraud Control Unit and to the HHS Office of Inspector General. These are criminal referrals with no cost to the complainant.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-006-05', 'Appeal of a denial of Medicaid HCBS waiver eligibility or a reduction in authorized service hours: state-specific, commonly 30-90 days from the notice. See also the Government Benefits pipelines.', 'MEDICAID CONTINUATION OF BENEFITS: where authorized service hours are being reduced or terminated, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS from the notice -- generally keeps the existing hours in place pending the hearing.', 'Request an Area Agency on Aging assessment at any time -- no deadline, no income test for many Older Americans Act services, and no cost. Apply to any HCBS waiting list immediately; position is dated from application.', 'Where a service authorization is reduced, request the assessment tool and scoring that produced the reduction. Assessment errors are the most common and most correctable cause of a service cut.', 'Judicial review of a final Medicaid fair hearing decision: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Older Americans Act services are generally not subject to a fair hearing process -- escalate through the state unit on aging instead.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-006-06', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Civil limitations periods are commonly 1-3 years from injury or discovery; wrongful death is frequently a separate and sometimes shorter period running from death.', 'Where a government entity is a defendant -- a county-operated facility, a public guardian, a public agency -- a NOTICE OF CLAIM deadline as short as 30 DAYS may apply before any suit is possible. Determine this on day one.', 'Report to law enforcement at any time. Criminal limitations periods for elder abuse offenses are frequently longer than civil ones, so a closed civil window does not necessarily close the criminal referral.', 'Where a prosecutor declines, request the declination in writing and refer to the Medicaid Fraud Control Unit and the state Attorney General separately. These are distinct offices with distinct charging authority.', 'Statutes of repose and capacity-based tolling may extend or curtail the civil window. Where the older adult lacked capacity, verify the tolling rule rather than applying the ordinary deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-01', 'Internal appeal of a benefit denial: policy- and state-specific, commonly 30-180 days from the denial letter. The denial must state the deadline -- if it does not, that omission is itself a complaint ground with the state Department of Insurance.', 'For a claim already being paid that the insurer is terminating, dispute IN WRITING BEFORE the termination date and ask that benefits continue pending review. Retroactive reinstatement is far harder than preventing a cutoff.', 'Request the insurer''s complete claim file, the assessment report it relied on, and the specific policy provision and internal criteria applied. Many states require production on request; ask in writing and cite the request as a precondition to a meaningful appeal.', 'Where the insurer''s assessment was brief, telephonic, or conducted by someone who never observed the insured, request an in-person reassessment and submit an independent functional and cognitive evaluation from the treating clinician.', 'Contractual suit-limitation clauses commonly require suit within 1-3 years, sometimes shorter than the state''s general contract statute. The policy controls -- read it before assuming the statute governs.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-02', 'Internal appeal: commonly 30-180 days from the denial. Submit the new evidence WITH the appeal rather than promising it -- appeals decided on the existing record are frequently upheld and the second level is harder.', 'If benefits were being paid and are being stopped, dispute before the stop date and request continuation during review. If care is being reduced because benefits stopped, document the resulting decline -- it is relevant evidence.', 'Request an IN-PERSON reassessment explicitly where the original was telephonic or brief. Where the insurer refuses, put the refusal in the record; it is a strong point in a Department of Insurance complaint.', 'Ask the insurer in writing to identify which specific ADLs or cognitive criteria it found the insured capable of, and the evidence for each. A denial that cannot answer that question line by line is vulnerable.', 'Where the policy is an ERISA-governed employer group product -- less common but it exists -- ERISA claims procedures apply instead, including a 180-day appeal right and free access to the entire claim file.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-03', 'Appeal of a lapse determination: commonly 30-60 days, but the REINSTATEMENT right is the operative remedy and has its own window -- commonly around five months from the lapse where cognitive impairment caused it.', 'REQUEST REINSTATEMENT IMMEDIATELY on discovering a lapse. The cognitive-impairment reinstatement window is measured in months and closes; after it closes, the argument shifts to whether the lapse was valid at all.', 'Ask the insurer in writing to produce: proof the third-party notice option was offered, the designation on file, and proof that lapse notice was actually sent to both the insured and any designee. Failure on any of these frequently voids the lapse.', 'Where reinstatement is denied, file with the state Department of Insurance and cite the cognitive-impairment reinstatement requirement specifically. This is a regulator-enforced provision and complaints on it are frequently effective.', 'Contractual suit-limitation clauses apply, commonly 1-3 years. State contract limitations periods may be longer but the policy generally controls.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-04', 'Internal appeal of a rescission: commonly 30-180 days from the notice. Raise the incontestability tier explicitly and early -- it is a legal argument the reviewer must address.', 'Where benefits were being paid and the insurer now seeks rescission, dispute before any termination date and request that benefits continue pending review.', 'Request the complete underwriting file and the application AS SUBMITTED, including any agent notes and the recorded telephone interview if one occurred. Compare what the insured actually said against what was recorded.', 'File a parallel complaint with the state Department of Insurance alleging post-claim underwriting where the insurer conducted no meaningful underwriting at issuance. This is a recognized regulatory violation, not merely a contract argument.', 'Contractual suit-limitation clauses apply. Where rescission is upheld, most states require refund of premiums paid -- demand it explicitly rather than assuming it is automatic.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-05', 'Department of Insurance complaint: file at any time and in parallel with the internal appeal. There is no exhaustion requirement and no fee.', 'Where benefits are being terminated, file the regulator complaint BEFORE the termination date and state the urgency. Regulator involvement before a cutoff is materially more effective than after.', 'Request the department''s file and the carrier''s written response to your complaint. The carrier''s written position is frequently more specific than its denial letter and creates points to attack.', 'If the department closes the complaint without a substantive review, request supervisory review in writing and ask for the carrier''s response and the reviewer''s basis.', 'Where the department finds no violation, the private contract remedy remains and runs on the policy''s suit-limitation clause, commonly 1-3 years. A regulator''s closure does not bar suit.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-007-06', 'Medicaid eligibility denial appeal: state-specific, commonly 30-90 days from the notice. VA benefit decisions: generally 1 YEAR to file a notice of disagreement or select a review lane -- see the Government Benefits pipelines.', 'MEDICAID CONTINUATION OF BENEFITS: where authorized services are being reduced or terminated, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS from the notice -- generally keeps existing services in place pending the hearing.', 'Apply to Medicaid and to any HCBS waiting list IMMEDIATELY, in parallel with the insurance appeal. Position on a waiver waiting list is dated from application, not from need.', 'Where a Medicaid application is denied for excess resources, ask about spousal impoverishment protections, permitted transfers, and whether a pooled or special needs trust applies. Denials on resource grounds are frequently correctable rather than final.', 'Judicial review of a final Medicaid fair hearing decision: state-specific, commonly 30 days. VA appeals follow their own multi-lane review structure with distinct deadlines.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-01', 'Not an appeal posture. Where a capacity determination is disputed, the route is an independent clinical evaluation, and ultimately a protective proceeding -- see the last entry in this dossier.', 'The right to refuse services persists as long as capacity does. APS closing a case on refusal does not prevent re-reporting later if conditions or capacity change -- and conditions frequently do change.', 'Request a capacity evaluation through the person''s own clinician where there is genuine concern. A clinician the person trusts obtains cooperation that a stranger sent by the family will not.', 'Where an evaluation is stale or was performed during an acute episode -- a hospitalization, an infection, a medication reaction -- request a repeat assessment at baseline. Delirium is routinely mistaken for permanent incapacity.', 'Protective proceedings are the last resort and carry their own standards and deadlines. See the Guardianship Abuse pipeline for less restrictive alternatives that should be exhausted first.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-02', 'Not an appeal posture. Where benefits are denied, standard benefit appeal deadlines apply -- see the Government Benefits pipelines.', 'A hospitalization or emergency department visit is an opportunity, not just a crisis. Ask for a geriatric consultation, a medication review, and a depression screen while the person is already being seen.', 'Request a comprehensive geriatric assessment. Ask the clinician specifically to evaluate for depression, delirium, medication effects, sensory loss, and executive function -- a general visit will not surface these.', 'Where cognitive decline is diagnosed, ask whether it is being managed and whether reversible contributors were excluded first. A dementia diagnosis made during an acute illness deserves a repeat assessment at baseline.', 'Medical records: HIPAA gives the person and their personal representative a right of access, generally within 30 days, enforceable free through HHS Office for Civil Rights.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-03', 'APS findings in self-neglect cases are generally not appealable by a reporter. Where a protective intervention is pursued, the person has full due process rights including counsel.', 'IF THERE IS IMMEDIATE DANGER -- no heat in winter, no water, fire hazard, untreated serious wound, no food -- call 911 or emergency services alongside APS. APS response timelines are measured in days; emergency services respond now.', 'Report by phone and follow in writing. Ask for the intake number. Emergency reports commonly draw a response within 24 hours; routine reports commonly within 10 days.', 'If a report is screened out, ask in writing what additional information would change the screening decision, and re-report on material change. Escalating patterns are tracked and do change outcomes.', 'Where APS pursues a protective proceeding, guardianship deadlines and rights apply -- notice, presence at hearing, counsel, independent evaluation, and the requirement that the court consider less restrictive alternatives.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-04', 'Appeal of a Medicaid HCBS eligibility denial or a reduction in authorized hours: state-specific, commonly 30-90 days from the notice. Benefit denials follow their own program appeal tracks.', 'MEDICAID CONTINUATION OF BENEFITS: where authorized hours are being reduced or terminated, requesting a fair hearing within the state''s continuation window -- commonly 10 DAYS from the notice -- generally keeps existing services in place pending the hearing.', 'Request an Area Agency on Aging assessment at any time; no deadline, no cost, and no income test for many services. Apply to any HCBS waiting list immediately -- position is dated from application.', 'Where a service authorization is reduced, request the assessment tool and scoring that produced it. Assessment error is the most common and most correctable cause of a service cut.', 'Judicial review of a final Medicaid fair hearing decision: state-specific, commonly 30 days. Older Americans Act services generally have no fair hearing process -- escalate through the state unit on aging instead.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-05', 'Eviction judgment appeal: state-specific and very short, commonly 5-30 days, frequently requiring a bond or ongoing rent deposit to stay the writ. Subsidized housing grievance hearings are commonly 10-14 days from the notice.', 'APPEARING ON THE COURT DATE IS THE CONTINUED-TENANCY MECHANISM. Non-appearance produces an immediate default judgment for possession. A reasonable accommodation request can be raised at the hearing even if not made earlier.', 'Request the reasonable accommodation IN WRITING as early as possible and request a hearing on any termination notice within the stated window. In subsidized housing that window is commonly 10-14 days.', 'Where an accommodation is denied, request the denial in writing with the reason, then file with HUD or the state fair housing agency. Refusal to engage in the interactive process is independently actionable.', 'HUD fair housing complaint: 1 year from the last discriminatory act. Private Fair Housing Act suit: 2 years. These run separately -- a HUD filing does not extend the court deadline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-008-06', 'Appeal of a guardianship order: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry. Petition to modify, narrow, terminate, or restore rights: NO DEADLINE, available at any time thereafter.', 'Emergency or temporary guardianship is TIME-LIMITED by statute in every state and requires a prompt full hearing -- commonly within days to a few weeks. Confirm the expiration date and the hearing date at the moment the temporary order issues.', 'The proposed protected person is entitled to a hearing, to be present, and to counsel. Request appointed counsel and an independent evaluation explicitly -- both are commonly available and rarely volunteered.', 'Where the court is inclined toward guardianship, ask in the alternative for a LIMITED order covering only the specific decisions at issue. Partial orders are granted far more often than families expect when they are actually requested.', 'Interstate guardianship jurisdiction and transfer are governed by UAGPPJA. An order entered without jurisdiction is challengeable on that basis alone.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-01', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Civil claims for fiduciary breach and conversion run on state limitations periods, commonly 2-6 years and frequently from discovery of the breach rather than the transaction.', 'If the principal still has capacity, they may revoke the POA immediately -- see the revocation entry. Revocation stops future acts; it does not undo past ones, which is why the accounting petition matters separately.', 'Where a POA affecting real property was recorded, obtain the recorded copy from the county recorder. The recorded version is authoritative and its recording date is frequently probative.', 'Where the POA appears forged, altered, or executed when the principal lacked capacity, the challenge is to the VALIDITY of the instrument itself rather than to the agent''s conduct -- a different and often stronger theory. Obtain the notary''s journal where the state requires one.', 'A will contest or trust challenge arising from the same conduct has its own and much shorter deadlines -- commonly measured in months from probate opening. Verify separately.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-02', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. Fiduciary breach claims run on state limitations periods, commonly 2-6 years, and in most states run from DISCOVERY of the breach -- which for a concealed self-dealing transaction can be years after it occurred.', 'Send a written demand for an accounting to the agent before petitioning. Some states require a demand first, and an agent''s refusal to respond to a written demand is itself evidence and strengthens the petition.', 'Petition to compel an accounting and to review the agent''s conduct: available in nearly every state WITHOUT first proving misconduct. See the next entry -- this is the central tool in this pipeline.', 'Where the agent produces an accounting, review it against the underlying bank records rather than accepting the summary. Discrepancies between the accounting and the statements are the substance of most successful petitions.', 'Where the agent has resigned or the principal has died, the claim survives against the agent personally and may be pursued by the successor agent, the personal representative of the estate, or in some states directly by an interested person.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-03', 'Appeal of a probate court order on a petition: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry of the order. Probate appeal rules frequently differ from ordinary civil rules -- verify with the clerk.', 'REQUEST SUSPENSION OF THE AGENT''S AUTHORITY AND AN ASSET FREEZE IN THE PETITION ITSELF where funds are actively moving. Courts can act before the accounting is produced, and an accounting delivered after the assets are gone is of limited value.', 'Hearing on the petition: set by the court, commonly within 30-60 days of filing, and sooner on an emergency showing. Ask the clerk about an expedited or ex parte track where assets are at immediate risk.', 'Where the agent''s accounting is inadequate or unsupported, move to compel production of the underlying records rather than accepting the summary. The gap between an accounting and the bank statements is where these cases are won.', 'Where the principal has died, the accounting demand generally passes to the personal representative of the estate and may be pursued in the probate proceeding. Probate deadlines are shorter -- verify immediately on death.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-04', 'Not an administrative appeal posture. A third party''s improper refusal to honor a valid POA is separately actionable under most state statutes, including for attorney fees.', 'Notify every institution IN WRITING and confirm each has updated its records. Ask each to confirm in writing that the agent''s access has been removed -- verbal confirmations routinely fail to propagate to branch systems.', 'Where the principal''s capacity to revoke is disputed, obtain a contemporaneous capacity evaluation from a clinician. A dated clinical assessment made at the time of revocation forecloses the argument later.', 'Where an institution refuses to accept a validly executed POA or a revocation, most state statutes require acceptance or a written statement of the reason within a defined period -- commonly 7 business days for acceptance and a further period after a certification is provided.', 'Where the principal lacks capacity to revoke and the agent is abusing the authority, revocation is not available -- the route is the petition to suspend and remove the agent, and where necessary a protective proceeding. See the accounting petition entry.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-05', 'APS findings are generally not appealable by a reporter. A person named in a substantiated finding commonly has a 30-day administrative appeal right.', 'Ask the institutions for holds and record preservation IMMEDIATELY. Branch and ATM surveillance is typically overwritten within 30-90 days and is frequently the only proof of who physically executed a transaction.', 'Unauthorized electronic transfers: Regulation E generally requires notice to the institution within 60 DAYS OF THE STATEMENT to preserve full protection. This runs from the statement date, not from discovery.', 'Where an institution declines to act, escalate in writing to compliance, then file with the CFPB, the state banking department, and for brokerages with FINRA and the state securities regulator.', 'Criminal limitations periods for elder financial exploitation are frequently longer than civil ones and in some states run from discovery. A closed civil window does not necessarily close the criminal referral.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-009-06', 'Appeal of a surcharge or removal order: state-specific, commonly 30 days from entry. Probate appeal rules frequently differ from ordinary civil rules.', 'Seek asset preservation before judgment -- attachment, lis pendens, injunction against transfer. A surcharge order against an agent who has already spent or moved the money is unenforceable in practice.', 'Where the principal has died, the claim survives and is generally pursued by the personal representative in probate. Probate claim deadlines are much shorter than civil ones -- commonly measured in months from the opening of the estate.', 'Where a successor agent steps in, request an immediate accounting from the outgoing agent as a condition of transition. The transition point is the last easy moment to obtain records.', 'Fraudulent transfer claims against downstream recipients run under the Uniform Voidable Transactions Act, commonly 4 years or 1 year from discovery, whichever is later.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-01', 'Regulation E unauthorized transfer claims: notice to the institution generally within 60 DAYS OF THE STATEMENT to preserve full protection. Fair Credit Billing Act billing error disputes: written notice generally within 60 days of the statement.', 'Ask the institution to place a HOLD on the account and to preserve records including branch and ATM video. Do not close the account before records are preserved -- closure sometimes complicates retrieval.', 'IC3 report: file the SAME DAY for any wire or crypto transfer. The Recovery Asset Team''s realistic freeze window is generally inside roughly 72 hours and is the single highest-value action here.', 'If the institution declines to act, escalate in writing to compliance, then file with the CFPB, the state banking department, and -- for brokerage transfers -- FINRA and the state securities regulator. Institutions respond differently to a regulator complaint.', 'Civil recovery against an identified perpetrator runs on the state fraud limitations period, commonly 2-6 years, frequently from discovery. Most scam recovery, realistically, happens through the payment channel rather than through litigation.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-02', 'Not an appeal posture. Report immediately regardless of elapsed time -- reports feed pattern databases even when individual recovery is no longer possible, and that is how these operations are eventually stopped.', 'If the scam is ONGOING -- the victim is still in contact and still sending -- that is the emergency. Say so explicitly to every agency, and involve APS if the victim does not believe it is a scam.', 'IC3 and FTC reports: file the same day. Police report: file promptly and get the number. Ask each agency for a case or report number and keep them together.', 'Where local police decline, request the declination in writing and refer to the state Attorney General and, where interstate wires or mail were used, to federal authorities. Wire and mail fraud are federal offenses.', 'Criminal limitations periods for fraud against older adults are frequently longer than civil ones and in many states run from discovery. A closed civil window does not close the criminal referral.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-03', 'Not an appeal posture. Where the victim is still engaged with the scammer, the priority is disengagement and payment channel protection rather than persuasion.', 'IF THE VICTIM DOES NOT BELIEVE IT IS A SCAM, do not force a confrontation that ends the conversation. Ask them to make one call to 877-908-3360 or 833-372-8311 with you present. A neutral third party is heard where family is not.', 'Where remote access to a computer was granted, disconnect it from the internet, have it professionally cleaned, and change every password from a DIFFERENT device. Assume email and banking credentials are compromised.', 'Where the victim may have unknowingly received and forwarded funds for someone else, they may have been used as a money mule. This carries real legal exposure -- disclose it to law enforcement early rather than after it is discovered.', 'Not applicable. Recognition work runs alongside every other track and is the primary defense against re-victimization.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-04', 'FCRA dispute reinvestigation: 30 days, extendable to 45. Identity theft BLOCK: 4 business days from receipt of a valid identity theft report and proof of identity -- much faster and much stronger.', 'Place the credit freeze BEFORE disputing anything, so no new accounts open during the reinvestigation window. The freeze is free, permanent until lifted, and must be placed separately at each bureau.', 'Request the business records created by anyone who opened an account in the victim''s name. Under the FCRA the business must generally provide them within 30 days on presentation of the identity theft report and proof of identity.', 'If a bureau rejects a block, it must notify you and state why, and you may resubmit with corrected documentation. A rejected block is usually a documentation defect, not a final answer.', 'FCRA private suit: generally the earlier of 2 years from discovery or 5 years from the violation. Willful violations carry statutory damages without proof of actual loss.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-05', 'Not an appeal posture. Report each new approach; repeat contact from the same operation is exactly what builds the enforcement case.', 'Expect the second wave and prepare for it BEFORE it arrives. Telling the victim in advance that a ''we found your money'' call is coming is far more effective than trying to stop it in progress.', 'Report every recovery-scam approach to the FTC and IC3 the same way as the original. These reports are how the victim-list trade is mapped.', 'Register on the National Do Not Call Registry and ask the phone carrier about call blocking and spam filtering. Consider changing the phone number where contact volume is heavy.', 'Not applicable. Re-victimization defense is continuous and is the highest-value long-term work in this pipeline.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18'),
('SAIS-EC-010-06', 'Crime victim compensation application: state-specific, commonly 1-3 years from the crime, with a shorter law enforcement reporting requirement -- frequently 72 hours to 5 days. Ask about good-cause exceptions, which are common and frequently granted.', 'Register as a victim in any criminal case promptly and keep contact information current with the prosecutor. Restitution cannot be paid to someone the case cannot locate.', 'Apply for victim compensation even where the loss appears to be purely financial -- counseling costs are covered in most states and are the most commonly needed and least claimed benefit.', 'Where a compensation claim is denied, most programs have an administrative appeal, commonly 30 days from the denial notice. Denials for missing the reporting window are frequently reversed on a good-cause showing.', 'Restitution orders are enforceable as judgments and are generally not dischargeable in the perpetrator''s bankruptcy. Collection is slow but the obligation persists for years.', 'luminari-SAIS-ALL-RECORDS-2026.csv', 'fb433b18')
ON CONFLICT (resource_id) DO NOTHING RETURNING resource_id) SELECT count(*) AS inserted FROM ins